# HTTPS for the dashboard, so browser notifications work away from `localhost`.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The Notification API requires a SECURE CONTEXT. `localhost` counts as one, so
# opening the dashboard on the machine itself always worked — but reaching it over the LAN
# (http://192.168.x.x:8801, or from a phone) does not, and the browser then refuses to let you
# enable notifications at all. That is the entire blocker; the feature itself is unchanged.
#
# `tls internal` mints certificates from a local CA Caddy manages. No public domain, no ACME, no
# port open to the internet — appropriate for a service that runs locally in a container. The cost
# is that the CA must be trusted once per device (see README).
#
# OPT-IN: runs only under `docker compose --profile https up -d`. A normal `docker compose up -d`
# never starts it, so nobody inherits a TLS layer they did not ask for.

{
	# Local CA only. Never attempts public ACME issuance, so this cannot leak a hostname to a
	# certificate-transparency log or fail because the host is unreachable from the internet.
	local_certs
	admin off
}

# THE SITE ADDRESSES MUST BE NAMED. A bare `:8443` looks tidier and does not work: Caddy has no
# name to issue a certificate FOR, so every handshake dies with TLSV1_ALERT_INTERNAL_ERROR and the
# browser just says the connection failed. Measured exactly that before naming them.
#
# USE HOSTNAMES, NOT BARE IPs — measured, and the reason is Docker. A client connecting to an IP
# sends NO SNI, so Caddy has to pick a certificate by the connection's local address. Behind
# Docker's port forward that address is the CONTAINER's IP (172.x), not the host's 192.168.x, so
# Caddy looks for a cert it was never asked to issue and the handshake dies with
# TLSV1_ALERT_INTERNAL_ERROR. Verified by probing the same port with three SNI values:
#   SNI=localhost        -> handshake OK
#   SNI=192.168.100.10   -> TLSV1_ALERT_INTERNAL_ERROR
#   SNI=none             -> TLSV1_ALERT_INTERNAL_ERROR
# Listing the IP as a site address does not help; Caddy issues that certificate happily and then
# never gets a chance to select it. A hostname is carried in SNI and survives the NAT.
#
# Override with HTTPS_SITES in .env. Every name you intend to REACH IT BY must be listed:
#   HTTPS_SITES=localhost:8443, stevenz-l.local:8443, aify.lan:8443
{$HTTPS_SITES:localhost:8443, 127.0.0.1:8443, stevenz-l:8443, stevenz-l.local:8443} {
	tls internal

	# The API and its WebSocket are proxied through the SAME origin on purpose, and this is
	# necessary rather than tidy: an https page cannot open a `ws://` socket or fetch an `http://`
	# API — mixed content is blocked — so without this the dashboard would load over TLS and then
	# fail to reach the service at all. The client resolves its API origin from the page origin by
	# default, so this needs no configuration on the dashboard side.
	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy service:8800
	}
	handle /ws {
		reverse_proxy service:8800
	}
	handle /version {
		reverse_proxy service:8800
	}

	# Everything else is the dashboard, including /assets/* and /health.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy new-dashboard:8801
	}

	encode gzip
}
